          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "calamitās, calamitātis — \"a disaster, a misfortune\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Post illam calamitātem multī cīvēs pauperēs erant. After that disaster many citizens were poor."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, 3rd declension. First harm to crops, then any disaster. English calamity."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "calamitās first meant \"loss, damage\", especially to crops (Lewis & Short); its origin is not settled. English calamity comes from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
